Node.js – Quick Viva Notes
1. OS – Operating System

An Operating System (OS) is system software that manages the computer’s hardware, software, files, and resources.

Examples: Windows, macOS, Linux.

2. Path

Path is used to specify the location of a file or folder in a computer.

In Node.js, the Path module helps us work with file and folder paths.

3. DNS – Domain Name System

DNS (Domain Name System) converts a domain name into its IP address.

Example:
google.com → IP address

It helps the browser find the correct server.

4. FS – File System

The FS (File System) module is used in Node.js to work with files and folders.

It can be used to:

Create files
Read files
Write/update files
Delete files
Rename files

DNS: Finds the IP address of a domain name.
FS: Works with files and folders.

5. HTTP – HyperText Transfer Protocol

HTTP is a protocol/rule set used for communication and data transfer between a client (browser) and a server.

HTTP methods include:

GET → Used to get/read data from the server.
POST → Used to send/create new data on the server.
PUT → Used to completely update existing data.
PATCH → Used to partially update existing data.
DELETE → Used to delete data from the server.
Difference between PUT and PATCH
PUT: Completely updates/replaces the resource.
PATCH: Updates only specific parts of the resource.
Browser and Postman

A web browser commonly sends GET requests when accessing web pages.

Postman is used to test APIs and can send different HTTP requests such as:
GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, and DELETE.

6. Process – process.argv

process.argv is used in Node.js to capture command-line arguments provided while running a program.

It allows us to take input/data from the CLI (Command Line Interface).

7. Crypto

The Crypto module in Node.js provides cryptographic functionality.

It can be used for:

Hashing
Encryption/decryption
Generating secure random values
Creating cryptographic keys
8. Types of Modules in Node.js

A module is a self-contained piece of code that can be reused in a Node.js application.

Core Modules
Built into Node.js.
No separate installation is required.
Examples: fs, path, http, dns, crypto.
Internal/Custom Modules
Modules created by the developer for their own application.
Used to organize and reuse application code.
External Modules
Modules created by other developers and available through npm.
Usually installed using npm install.
Example: is-even.
9. Crypto

The Crypto module is commonly used for hashing and encryption of data.

Hashing: Converts data into a fixed-length value and is generally one-way.
Encryption: Converts data into an encoded form that can be decrypted using the appropriate key.

10. CLI – Command Line Interface

CLI allows users to interact with a computer/program by entering commands in a terminal.

In Node.js, CLI can also be used for capturing input through command-line arguments, such as using process.argv.

11. Router

A Router is used to organize and manage different routes/endpoints of an application.

It helps keep routes clean, structured, and separate.

Example routes:

/users
/products
/login
12. Installing Node.js and Creating a Node.js Project

Step 1: Initialize a Node.js application
npm init -y

Step 2: Install an external module
npm i is-even

Step 3: Import/require the module into the program.

Step 4: Use the module according to your requirement.

Important: npm is the Node Package Manager, used to install and manage packages.

13. CommonJS vs ES Modules
CommonJS	ES Modules
Uses require()	Uses import
Uses module.exports	Uses export
Traditional Node.js module system	Modern JavaScript module system

In short:
CommonJS → require / module.exports
ES Modules → import / export

14. Express.js

Express.js is a minimal and flexible web framework for Node.js.

It is mainly used to create:

Web servers
REST APIs
Routes
Backend applications

Express is unopinionated, meaning it does not force developers to follow one specific project structure or way of doing things.

15. JSON – JavaScript Object Notation

JSON stands for JavaScript Object Notation.

It is a lightweight and commonly used data interchange/transfer format between clients and servers.

JSON.stringify()

Converts a JavaScript object into a JSON string.

Object → JSON string

JSON.parse()

Converts a JSON string into a JavaScript object.

JSON string → JavaScript object

